Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
hello to you ms sturgeon thanks for  joining us also we put it to labor this  morning about the promise or hope for a  second referendum in the form of lisa  nandy she said you've got some brassneck  what would you say in response  i'm a democrat i was elected first  minister my government was elected at  just over a year ago on a proposition to  give people in scotland the choice of  independence recognizing at the  significant changes that have taken  place in the uk since scotland last had  that opportunity recognising the fact  that westminster with governments we  don't vote for here in scotland taking  us in the wrong direction there is a  majority clear majority in the scottish  parliament behind that proposition so  it's a matter of democracy that people  should have the right to choose and then  whether or not people choose  independence or not is a matter not for  politicians whether that's me ben  wallace or lisa and andy that's a matter  for the scottish people and i suspect if  my political opponents  were  actually believed what they say that  scotland doesn't want independence then  they would not be so desperate to block  scotland's opportunity to have that  choice  well he said in 2014 as we all know that  this was a once in a generation um  referendum i wonder what the clear  mandate is that you're talking about i  was looking in the daily record saying  that there have been 23 polls in the  last year and yes has led in only three  of them  well let me take head on this once in  the generation point in in 2014 at the  last referendum now almost a decade ago  those of us arguing for independence yes  said to people make sure you don't lose  this opportunity we might never get the  chance again but no politician can stand  in the way of democracy democracy is not  a single fixed moment in time people in  a democracy have the right to change  their minds if circumstances change and  nobody can argue that circumstances have  not changed since 2014. you know back  then scotland was told we would lose  european union membership if we voted  for independence and now we're out of  the european union because we didn't  become independent that's happened  against our will we were told the  prospect of boris johnson ever becoming  prime minister was ridiculous  that may have been true but it is  nevertheless a reality now and yet again  we have a westminster government pulling  us in the wrong direction creating the  worst cost of living crisis in the  developed world almost because of brexit  and scotland is expected to just say  well you know there's nothing we can do  about that so this is about democratic  choice it's not up to me whether  scotland becomes independent but it is  up to me to fulfill the promise i made  to people in scotland and was elected on  to offer people that choice and you can  quote opinion polls i can quote opinion  polls back at you but what i'm actually  standing on is the outcome of a  democratic election uh there is a  stronger mandate  for an independent referendum in the  scottish parliament than there ever was  for a brexit referendum in the uk  parliament a majority of msps in the  scottish parliament support a referendum  so if we are a democracy then that  should be respected and then of course  it is up to people in scotland whether  they vote for independence or against  independence that is how to resolve  issues in any democracy  i was speaking to the government  minister yesterday and he said chris  phelp he was saying that what you should  be doing is uh concentrating more on  drug deaths in scotland and we took the  opportunity to look at the figures drug  deaths in scotland 35 times worse than  they are in france 15 times worse than  they are in germany that's where you  should be focused  we are focused on that i want to come on  in a second to the the link between  independence and some of these  day-to-day issues that are priorities  for people but we are focused on these  things every  single day um there are  long  reasons with deep roots in terms of  scotland's drug death situation but it  is unacceptable so we're investing at  250 million pounds in better treatment  faster treatment better rehabilitation  better community support to turn that  around i've appointed a dedicated  minister to lead this work we are taking  this seriously of course one of the  things that there is across party  support around in scotland and we're  trying to find a way to do it is to  establish something that has been shown  to work in other countries which is safe  consumption facilities we're unable or  have been unable to to do that  straightforwardly through a change in  legislation because the misuse of drugs  act is reserved to the westminster  parliament so i'm not seeking to escape  responsibility on the the drugs death  issue but even on that the fact that the  scottish parliament is not independent  it has real implications but finally kay  on this issue of it's a choice between  focusing on day-to-day priorities or  focusing on independence independence is  about better equipping scotland to deal  with and navigate the challenges we face  we're struggling with a cost of living  crisis right now which is worse in the  uk than it is in any other g7 country  precisely because of brexit something  imposed on scotland against our will my  government is investing at almost three  billion pounds a year to help people  with the cost of living we've  established a scottish child payment  doesn't exist anywhere else in the uk  and yet as we do that we have a  chancellor at the stroke of his pen  pushing more children into poverty by  taking money away from university has  been rising independence is about giving  okay child poverty has changed scotland  a million people on the nhs waiting list  let me can i can i take on that and can  i take it i'll come to the nhs in a  second yeah  the child poverty point  i need to address because firstly child  poverty in scotland is the lowest  of all of the four uk uh nations i think  in the last  statistics and that is because of the  action we're taking we established a  scottish child payment 20 pounds a week  to the lowest income families no other  government in the uk has done that but  child poverty is rising because so many  of the decisions that determine that are  taken at westminster so cuts to benefits  at 20 pounds a week being taken away  from low-income families in the  universal credit claw back so that's an  argument for equipping the scottish  parliament with all of the powers that  countries across the world who are  already independent take for granted and  you know we published analysis just a  couple of weeks ago showing that a range  of countries comparable to scotland many  smaller than us uh most of them without  the resources that scotland okay talk to  me about the energy better economically  and socially because they have the  powers of independence on the nhs the  nhs again waiting times in the nhs in  scotland accident emergency for example  shorter and in many ways doing better  than the other countries in the uk nhs  staff the highest paid in scotland in  the whole of the uk we have significant  problems in our national health service  largely because of the covert pressures  they are not unique to scotland but we  are supporting the nhs into and through  recovery but one of the things we are  struggling with is a situation where  westminster governments and its whales  in northern ireland are suffering from  this as well as are parts of england  budgets that are being cut by a  chancellor  who is pursuing austerity politics  rather than investing so we will be  better able to deal with  support for our public services if we  are in charge of all  of the levers that effectively determine  the environment and the financial  situation that we are operating within  worst ever scores in maths and science  in 20 years  sorry i didn't hear your question worst  ever scores in maths and science in 20  years  we're again like all countries we are  focused on these uh challenges but you  take the attainment gap for example at  narrowing again like most countries  exacerbated by covid but if you take the  attainment gap in access to university  which of course is a key measure  of how  schools are are performing often we have  an independent commissioner for fair  access to universities who in his report  just a couple of weeks ago said that  scotland was leading the way in the uk  that what we had done was an unequivocal  success and we are narrowing in fact  we're meeting our targets to narrow the  gap between the young people from  deprived communities and wealthier  communities in terms of the  opportunities to get to university so  using the powers we have we're not  shying away from challenges each and  every day we focus on these challenges  but my argument is we will be better  able to meet those challenges and to  meet the wider challenges that scotland  in common with countries around the  world face if we don't have one hand  tied behind our back and too often are  being pulled in the wrong direction by  westminster governments that we don't  vote for  but it's up to the supreme court isn't  it and they are unlikely to find in your  favor for a second referendum well  i mean i think i think we should allow  the supreme court to do  its job and i respect the rule of law  it's not the prerogative of government  referendum that's the point isn't it and  that's what they'll find well sorry  that the whole point the whole point of  making is that that is contested but you  cannot say that as a statement of fact  because it hasn't been judicially  determined that's what we are  through the decision of the lord  advocate in scotland are seeking to get  at judicial determination of that point  it wouldn't be necessary of course if  boris johnson and the uk government  simply respected democracy and agreed  the process to put a referendum beyond  legal doubt but i'm doing the  responsible thing a referendum has to be  lawful i'm not like boris johnson who's  of course breaching international law  with his actions on the northern ireland  protocol i respect the rule of law which  is why we are taking the path that i set  out yesterday that's the responsible  thing to do but i also respect democracy  scottish democracy cannot be a prisoner  of boris johnson or any uk prime  minister at the people of scotland must  have the right to choose it is then  entirely up to the people of scotland  what that choice is but trying to simply  block democracy as  unionist politicians are doing simply  because they fear the verdict of the  scottish people is not democratic it's  not acceptable and ultimately it is  simply not sustainable  always a pleasure miss sturgeon thank  you

